<section class="about-story" aria-labelledby="about-story-title">
  <div class="about-story__intro">
    <h2 id="about-story-title">A filmmaker with a marketing background.</h2>
    <p>I’m Peter Gyarmathy, the person behind NextVision Studio. I plan, film and edit video for businesses, with photography and related marketing services when the brief needs them. My work is based around Dublin and North Dublin, and my portfolio also includes projects in Hungary and Spain.</p>
    <p>I like starting with something concrete: a customer question, a process that is difficult to explain, a place with a particular atmosphere or a product detail worth noticing. That gives the production a purpose before we choose a format. You can see how those starting points become finished work in the <a href="/case-studies/">project case studies</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="about-story__body">
    <section>
      <h2>What I studied, and how it shapes the work</h2>
      <p>I studied Trade and Marketing at Budapest Business University, completing a BSc, and continued with a master’s degree in Management and Leadership. Those studies gave me a business perspective that I still bring into a production conversation: who is the customer, what are they trying to decide, and what does the business need to communicate?</p>
      <p>I later developed my creative work through study in the New York metropolitan area, including social media content, photography and video at Hudson County Community College. The college is in New Jersey, within the wider New York area. That experience added practical visual work to my marketing background and helped shape the direction I wanted to take as a photographer and filmmaker.</p>
      <p>Before building my freelance work, I also worked in social media at RAW Marketing Agency and in team leadership at Decathlon. Those experiences made the practical side of communication familiar: planning content, working with people, keeping a brief understandable and considering what happens after an asset is delivered. My creative decisions are still connected to those everyday business needs.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The main service: video people can understand</h2>
      <p>Video production is the centre of NextVision. I help businesses turn their knowledge, products and character into something a viewer can follow. That may be a series of short answers, a service explanation, a product film, an interview or an event story. The appropriate format depends on what the audience needs to see and where the video will be used.</p>
      <p><a href="/short-form-video-production-dublin/">Short-form video production</a> suits focused social pieces and campaign creative. <a href="/corporate-video-production-dublin/">Corporate and business video</a> gives a service, team or customer story more room. <a href="/event-videographer-dublin/">Event videography</a> follows a live occasion and shapes selected moments into finished work. For businesses needing a recurring supply, <a href="/social-media-content-creation-dublin/">monthly social content production</a> can bring planning and filming into a regular schedule.</p>
      <p>In the <a href="/case-studies/perfect-clean-video-advertising/">Perfect Clean project</a>, the owner’s own voice and the cleaning process supported a customer-focused message. In the <a href="/case-studies/balbriggan-braces-dental-videos/">Balbriggan Braces work</a>, the task was patient education across long and short videos. The work looks different because the people, questions and intended uses are different.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Photography with a defined use</h2>
      <p>Photography can help a customer inspect a product, recognise a team, picture a place or understand an event. I ask where the images will appear before deciding what needs to be photographed. A website gallery, a social post and a photograph printed for a wall call for different attention to framing, detail and delivery.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/case-studies/uri-muri-bar-video-photography/">Úri Muri project</a> combined image photographs for the bar with drinks videos. The <a href="/case-studies/dio-dublin-preschool-photography/">Dió Dublin brief</a> required warmth and activity to be communicated through details without faces in the selected work. At a <a href="/case-studies/saas-networking-photography-dublin/">Dublin SaaS networking evening</a>, the focus was conversation, relationships and the Irish setting. Each is a different use of the <a href="/photography-dublin/">photography service</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What I pay attention to on location</h2>
      <p>I enjoy working with the light and character a place already has. A room, a person’s usual way of explaining something or a small action can give a film its strongest material. I look for those details while keeping the brief in mind. Natural-looking work still needs decisions about timing, background, sound and what the viewer should notice first.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/case-studies/red-line-events-techno-video/">Red Line Events films</a> involved a particular challenge: club lighting that changed constantly. The <a href="/case-studies/si-ink-tattoo-halloween-video-gyor/">Si Ink Halloween film</a> called for spontaneity and improvisation. In the <a href="/case-studies/saxoo-london-fashion-video/">Saxoo London collection work</a>, elegance and a sense of mystery guided the choices. These projects are useful examples of how I adapt the production to a real creative problem.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Dublin work and international projects</h2>
      <p>I’m based in Balbriggan and work with businesses across Dublin and North Dublin. The <a href="/areas/">service area page</a> covers practical arrangements for places including Swords, Malahide and Balbriggan. Other locations are discussed as part of the brief, with travel and production requirements agreed before booking.</p>
      <p>My international work includes <a href="/case-studies/buga-ramen-alicante-restaurant-video/">Buga Ramen in Alicante</a>, where the task was to communicate Japanese-inspired atmosphere and food, and <a href="/case-studies/pekado-kebab-alicante-video/">Pekado Kebab</a>, with attention to a new business and its offering. The <a href="/case-studies/macaron-boutique-product-video/">Macaron Boutique film</a> follows a different pace and visual character. These experiences keep me attentive to what makes an individual business recognisable.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Content, advertising and a way to handle enquiries</h2>
      <p>A finished video can be the start of a wider plan. A business may also need help getting the content in front of people, giving those people a useful page to visit and organising the enquiries that follow. I can discuss those needs alongside production through <a href="/google-ads-meta-ads-dublin/">Google and Meta Ads management</a>, <a href="/webdesigner-dublin/">website design</a> and <a href="/crm-lead-automation-dublin/">CRM and lead automation</a>.</p>
      <p>The scope is agreed around the business’s actual process. If a team already has a website, campaign or CRM, the work can begin with the part that needs attention. The <a href="/case-studies/ventsolve-content-production/">VentSolve relationship</a> includes video, photography, CRM work and automation, showing how several services can support one business over time. The <a href="/#how-we-work">three-stage process on the home page</a> explains that connected approach.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What you receive at the end</h2>
      <p>The finished work should fit the use we agreed at the beginning. For video, the proposal defines the number of edits, their orientation, the intended channels and any captions, graphics or alternate versions. For photography, it identifies the subjects, image count and delivery requirements. Those details make it clear what your team will have available when the project is complete.</p>
      <p>Planning also gives the files somewhere useful to go. A service explanation may belong beside the relevant information on your website. A product detail may help introduce a collection. A group of short edits can give your team several topics to publish over time. The <a href="/blog/customer-questions-into-business-videos/">customer-question guide</a> gives examples of how a business can turn everyday conversations into content with a defined job.</p>
      <p>Before we start, we also agree how feedback is gathered and what changes the quote includes. One clear set of notes helps keep the review useful. The final handover then reflects the brief, with publishing or campaign work included when it has been explicitly scoped.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Helping the person in front of the camera</h2>
      <p>Many business owners are comfortable explaining their work to a customer and less comfortable explaining it to a lens. The production can begin with a familiar question and a conversation. A person who knows the service has useful material already; the task is to help that answer become clear on screen.</p>
      <p>A process, product or familiar workplace can support the explanation. In the <a href="/case-studies/amber-oxygen-therapy-video-production/">Amber Oxygen Therapy project</a>, a series of short videos and supporting photographs gave the business several pieces of content around its service. The format should make the knowledge easier for a viewer to approach, with enough context to understand what the next step involves.</p>
      <p>If you are unsure which format to request, describe the customer and the question they need answered. We can then discuss whether photographs, a short series or one fuller film fits the subject.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How we work together</h2>
      <p>You work directly with me on the creative brief and production decisions. I begin by understanding the audience, message, intended channels and required outputs. We then agree the filming or photography requirements, delivery, review process and usage. When a project needs additional crew or a specialist, that requirement belongs in the proposal.</p>
      <p>You do not need to arrive with a finished script. A real customer question, a product to introduce or an example of the problem you want to explain is enough to begin. The <a href="/blog/business-video-production-planning-dublin/">business video planning guide</a> helps organise those details, and the <a href="/blog/compare-business-video-quotes/">quote comparison checklist</a> shows which inclusions to look for before committing to a production.</p>
      <p>For published options, see the <a href="/how-much-does-a-videographer-cost-in-dublin/">NextVision pricing guide</a> and the <a href="/#content-packages">monthly video packages</a>. If you want to discuss your own project, <a href="/contact/#contact-form">send me the business, the location and what you want the work to help people understand</a>. I will use that to recommend a production scope and a practical next step.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>

